"Fire—!!"

A desperate cry from a royal soldier rang out, and panic spread like wildfire through the royal guesthouse.

What they witnessed was thick, billowing smoke pouring from the kitchen with tremendous force. Faced with this massive volume of smoke, hot and reeking of scorching, many of the brave soldiers immediately sprang into action.

They rushed into the kitchen to identify the fire’s source, urgently trying to determine the cause of the smoke filling the area. Depending on the situation, they intended to extinguish the flames and quell both the fire and the panic in short order.

However—

"The fire's source is under the floor, and the cause of the smoke is a blocked chimney... Putting it out won't be easy."

So said Petra, the very one who had proposed the fire alarm strategy and devised its detailed method. She smiled with deepening satisfaction as she listened to the panic unfold exactly as planned.

It was, by all normal standards, a smile that should have been called adorable, yet Garfiel, seeing it up close, couldn't shake an inexplicable chill down his spine.

At any rate—

"I really can't accept having 'setting buildings on fire' treated as my specialty!"

Setting aside Otto's protest, the fire alarm strategy was a resounding success.

Unable to risk endangering an important building like the royal guesthouse or the persons of interest confined within it, the royal soldiers were wholly occupied dealing with the smoke, their attention diverted from Garfiel and the others' movements.

In that window—

"Sorry 'bout this, but just take a little nap for us!"

"I apologize. My grudge... is only on behalf of my sister."

"—!"

Garfiel and Rem each took responsibility for the two soldiers who had remained at their posts guarding the back door at a minimum, silently subduing them and securing the path. Without making them draw their weapons. Fulfilling the conditions Otto had set beforehand, Rem and Garfiel exchanged glances and nodded to each other.

After that, they slipped outside the building together with Otto, Petra, and Meili.

"If ya wanted to cause a commotion, I could've done anythin' with my mabeast buddies, y'know."

"We absolutely cannot do that. If Meili-chan called mabeasts and had them rampage, it would just reinforce the suspicions against us. 'Subaru is a Witch Cult Archbishop,' 'Lady Emilia has ties to the Witch of Envy,' 'Meili-chan goes on rampages with mabeasts,' 'Otto-san burns down buildings left and right.'"

"Regarding that last suspicion, just like with the mansion before, there's counterevidence that I wasn't the only one present—Petra-chan was there too!"

"Whatever Otto-nii's nature may be, I got it."

Scurrying along with her skirt fluttering, Meili shrugged.

Still, despite her airy attitude, Garfiel figured that what she'd just said stemmed from a sense of frustration unique to Meili.

Meili had power. But it was a double-edged sword that, if wielded, would endanger even those she wanted to protect. The existence of a blade she couldn't swing undoubtedly left her feeling vexed.

"Don'tcha worry. I'll do the work to make up for what ya lack."

Running alongside her, Garfiel patted Meili on the head. Instantly, she gave him a withering glare, and Garfiel gnashed his fangs.

"Bro Otto's the ears, and I'm the nose. We'll sniff out the boss somehow. Till then, yeah... just cheer us on! Cheer! Sit tight and cheer."

"Cheer... like, 'do your best'?"

"That's it. And it really does give ya strength, bein' cheered on."

Meili eyed him doubtfully, but it was a sensation Garfiel remembered well.

After all, back in Priestella—where they were supposed to be heading—the reason Garfiel could even somewhat hold his own against Kurgan the 'Eight-Armed,' who had become a Corpse Soldier, was thanks to the younger siblings present there—and the cheers of the citizens who had been given hope, pushed forward by Subaru's speech.

Yes, that was it. Garfiel won that fight because of the power of those cheers. And the spirit to give those cheers existed precisely because Subaru had roused the people.

It was the result of Natsuki Subaru overwriting the despair the Witch Cult had prepared, using hope.

"There ain't no way that boss of ours is some damn Archbishop...!"

The very thought of such a suspicion was so utterly ridiculous that it infuriated Garfiel to no end, feeling as though the back of the man he respected had been smeared with filth.

And it wasn't just Priestella. There were plenty of people who knew of Natsuki Subaru's goodness—those from the 'Sanctuary' where Garfiel lived with his grandmother, the people of Petra's Arlam Village saved from the Witch Cult, and even those who took on the White Whale alongside Subaru.

—And the one who believed in Subaru the most strongly among them was none other than Emilia.

From what Otto said, the request for Emilia to come to the castle was aimed at confining her from the very start, with the questioning to clear Subaru's suspicions being nothing more than a pretense.

But if, by any chance, the other side genuinely possessed the will to clear Subaru of suspicion, then there was no one other than Emilia who could pour her entire soul into weaving the words to do so.

"Damn it."

Frustrating. Frustrating. Just so frustrating. So frustrating, so endlessly frustrating that it wouldn't stop.

Hadn't Subaru always given it his all? Wanting to be of use to Emilia, the woman he fell for; wanting to protect the hearts of everyone around him—hadn't he just kept struggling single-mindedly with all his might?

So why wouldn't they listen to the voices of those who knew of Subaru's efforts?

With just that one chance, they would surely understand.

—That Natsuki Subaru was the one who, striving with all his might to make an impossibly grand ideal a reality, was the absolute coolest, the big brother Garfiel was so proud of.

"Garf-nii..."

"—Tch, it's nothin'! 'Kurgan lost his arms and still took down his enemy'! I ain't got time to mope around!"

Though he'd meant to cheer Meili up, he found his own emotions welling up instead. He shook it off with a fierce bark and a gnash of his teeth, facing forward.

Seeing this, Meili narrowed her eyes slightly and murmured something that, to Garfiel, could only be taken as a compliment.

"You all really are Subaru-nii and Emilia-nee's comrades, huh."

"—Otto-san, what do we do from here?"

With the exchange between Garfiel and the others behind her, Rem ran up alongside Otto, who was in the lead, and posed that question.

In response, Otto guided Garfiel's group onward with "This way," while explaining:

"For now, I'd like to find somewhere to hide and avoid being tracked. It won't take long before our escape is discovered. I want to leave the noble district quickly. And to do that, I'm borrowing their help right now."

"—. —. I see."

Selecting back alleys and concealed paths, Otto led the route after their escape from the guesthouse. Ahead of Otto's gaze, those familiar insects were stealthily creeping about.

At the mention of borrowing their power, Rem limited her response to a simple acknowledgment. Garfiel had been burned badly in the past too, and had quite unpleasant memories of Otto negotiating with insects—mainly the Zoddabugs.

That said, with them on your side, there were few people—or insects—more reliable.

"If we get caught at a checkpoint, it's over. So—Garfiel!"

"Got it! You two, hold on tight!"

"Kya—!"

At Otto's call, Garfiel swept up Meili and Petra, who were right beside him, tucking one under each arm. He then kicked off the wall in front of them—the sturdy defensive wall connecting the royal capital's noble district and commoner district—and vaulted to the other side.

A wall that played a crucial role in the royal capital's security, but with Garfiel's leg strength, it was child's play.

"Wait here a sec. Gotta go bring Bro Otto and Rem over too."

"I get that... but Garf-san, you can't just suddenly scoop up girls. You startled me just now."

"Hah? What're ya worryin' 'bout somethin' so dumb for—don't give me the scary eyes! Fine, I got it!"

Seemingly a serious matter, Garfiel was cowed by Petra's glare and apologized. Then, with a running start, he glared up at the wall, ready to leap over again—

"—I'm sorry. We made it over without you, Garf."

At those words, Garfiel's eyes went wide at the sight of Rem touching down right in front of him. It was surprising that she'd managed to leap over this wall, but what surprised him even more was—

"Otto-nii managed to get over without screaming, huh."

"Dangerously close, though... Rem-san, please put me down. Natsuki-san will kill me."

"Huh? Why is that person's name coming up? I don't understand."

"That phrasing is exactly what someone who does understand would say!"

Glared at from close range by Rem, who was holding him aloft, Otto cried out. Watching Otto finally get set down on the ground somehow, and Petra teasing him about it, Garfiel let out a long breath and suppressed the emotions that had been raging just moments ago.

The situation was undeniably bad. Perhaps even worse than when they'd gotten caught up in the civil war in the Empire.

But Subaru was surely safe with Beatrice. Emilia, with Ram by her side, would do her best without being reckless. And right here, they had Otto, Rem, Petra, Meili—all of them reliable. —And he would support them all.

The situation was bad. But he could still smile. If he could smile, they weren't completely cornered yet.

"Bro Otto, next time I'm carryin' ya over when we fly."

"Yes, I'll be counting on you."

"Right. So where to from here? Somethin' 'bout hidin' out for a bit?"

"Indeed. For now, I'll guide us somewhere unpopulated—"

Swaying his head slowly, Otto had been scanning the surroundings, but then cut himself off. He blinked rapidly, then hastily cast his gaze around the buildings and shadows near the defensive wall, here and there.

Garfiel caught the scent of poorly concealed agitation.

"Bro Otto?"

"—They're gone."

"Gone? What's gone...?"

"The Zoddabugs. No, not just the Zoddabugs. All the other insects I'd entrusted with guiding us here have all vanished... Don't tell me!"

Eyes flying wide open, Otto turned back, not to a hiding place, but to face Garfiel and the whole group.

His expression stiff, his voice strained with urgency, he grabbed Garfiel's arm.

And then—

"I messed up! Somehow... the insects deceived me!"

"Insects, deceive... Deceive you, Otto-san? Something like that..."

"It is possible! We were led here! At this rate, there's no escape! We need to leave right now—"

The boundary of what they'd naturally assumed was a safe zone had been breached. Even Otto, the one directly involved, seemed unable to believe it, yet he urgently warned his companions of the worst possible scenario.

Taking this in, Garfiel forcibly shook off his own encroaching shock, preparing to act immediately on Otto's words—

"—That's far enough."

It was precisely at that moment that the worst possible pursuer caught up with them.
